1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:03,166
Think about the last
door you walked through.

2
00:00:03,233 --> 00:00:06,441
Now picture a town where nobody has one.

3
00:00:06,508 --> 00:00:08,155
There aren't even streets.

4
00:00:08,222 --> 00:00:11,151
Just flat roofs, wall against wall.

5
00:00:11,218 --> 00:00:12,151
To get home,

6
00:00:12,218 --> 00:00:16,797
you walk across your neighbours' roofs
and climb down a ladder through a hole.

7
00:00:16,864 --> 00:00:21,282
And under your floor lie the dead.
They were your family, everyone assumed.

8
00:00:21,349 --> 00:00:27,497
In 2025, the DNA of 131 of them
told a stranger story.

9
00:00:27,564 --> 00:00:29,966
This is Çatalhöyük, in Turkey.

10
00:00:30,033 --> 00:00:34,786
1,150 years of houses
built on top of houses.

11
00:00:34,853 --> 00:00:38,205
You're about to live here,
about 9,000 years ago.

12
00:00:38,272 --> 00:00:39,572
Would you survive it?

13
00:00:40,556 --> 00:00:44,343
Here's the game.
Five moments from your life in this town.

14
00:00:44,410 --> 00:00:48,282
Before each one, make your guess.
Would you make it through?

15
00:00:48,417 --> 00:00:52,856
And on the right, five things the world
has been told about this place.

16
00:00:52,923 --> 00:00:55,856
Let's see which of them
survive the evidence.

17
00:00:55,923 --> 00:01:00,139
You're born in a mud-brick house,
packed into the middle of the town.

18
00:01:00,206 --> 00:01:05,065
It's the town's busiest era,
about 8,600 years ago.

19
00:01:05,132 --> 00:01:07,728
Göbekli Tepe, where our journey began,

20
00:01:07,795 --> 00:01:12,466
was abandoned more than a thousand
years ago, 300 miles to the east.

21
00:01:12,533 --> 00:01:15,860
First question.
Do you make it through childhood?

22
00:01:15,996 --> 00:01:17,510
Make your guess.

23
00:01:18,621 --> 00:01:20,197
Look around the rooftops.

24
00:01:20,264 --> 00:01:23,704
Nearly one person in three is a child
under five.

25
00:01:23,771 --> 00:01:27,373
Babies are everywhere,
and so are their graves.

26
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,726
Infants were buried right
inside the house, often beside the hearth.

27
00:01:31,793 --> 00:01:36,151
In some homes, most of the graves
under the floor belong to children.

28
00:01:36,218 --> 00:01:39,080
And the ones who grew up carried a mark.

29
00:01:39,147 --> 00:01:44,015
Almost every adult here has faint
lines across the enamel of their teeth.

30
00:01:44,082 --> 00:01:47,836
Each line is a moment in childhood
when the teeth stopped forming

31
00:01:47,903 --> 00:01:51,053
properly for a while. Sickness, or hunger.

32
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:54,480
Yet the survivors reached
normal height for their time.

33
00:01:54,547 --> 00:01:59,109
Verdict: you make it.
With your childhood written on your teeth.

34
00:01:59,272 --> 00:02:02,143
Now,
how many people are you growing up with?

35
00:02:02,287 --> 00:02:06,793
For decades the answer was huge.
Up to 8,000.

36
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:11,510
Then, in 2024,
two archaeologists counted again.

37
00:02:11,577 --> 00:02:14,478
Not
every house was lived in at the same time.

38
00:02:14,545 --> 00:02:18,130
Most were homes for one generation,
then rebuilt.

39
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:20,076
One of them compared the old counts

40
00:02:20,143 --> 00:02:24,682
to assuming every airport hotel
ever built is full, all at once.

41
00:02:24,749 --> 00:02:28,987
Their estimate:
600 to 800 people in a normal year.

42
00:02:29,428 --> 00:02:31,964
Other studies
still use the bigger numbers.

43
00:02:32,031 --> 00:02:34,819
For now, the count has a question mark.

44
00:02:34,886 --> 00:02:37,485
What experts do agree on is this.

45
00:02:37,552 --> 00:02:40,118
Nobody has ever found a palace here.

46
00:02:40,185 --> 00:02:43,019
And no house much
richer than its neighbours.

47
00:02:43,086 --> 00:02:44,812
No sign of a ruler.

48
00:02:44,879 --> 00:02:47,666
So call it a town, or a big village.

49
00:02:47,733 --> 00:02:50,166
By most definitions, it was no city.

50
00:02:50,233 --> 00:02:54,285
Second question.
Could you stand living in your own house?

51
00:02:54,415 --> 00:02:55,929
Make your guess.

52
00:02:57,046 --> 00:02:59,621
Climb down the ladder and look around.

53
00:02:59,688 --> 00:03:01,644
The walls are bright white.

54
00:03:01,711 --> 00:03:06,281
Somebody keeps plastering
them with fresh clay, again and again.

55
00:03:06,348 --> 00:03:09,066
Probably because the oven
sits by the ladder,

56
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:12,486
and the only way
out for the smoke is up the same hole.

57
00:03:12,553 --> 00:03:16,450
On the wall, a wild bull's skull,
plastered over,

58
00:03:16,517 --> 00:03:18,847
its horns pointing straight at you.

59
00:03:18,914 --> 00:03:24,127
On another, painted hunters swarm
around a bull many times their size.

60
00:03:24,194 --> 00:03:27,423
And on one wall, someone painted this.

61
00:03:28,056 --> 00:03:31,482
About 80 little boxes,
packed like a honeycomb.

62
00:03:31,549 --> 00:03:34,552
Above them, a mountain with two peaks.

63
00:03:34,619 --> 00:03:38,350
Some see the town itself,
under an erupting volcano.

64
00:03:38,417 --> 00:03:40,779
The oldest map ever drawn.

65
00:03:40,846 --> 00:03:45,526
In 2014, geologists dated
pumice from the summit of Hasan Dağı,

66
00:03:45,593 --> 00:03:48,671
a twin-peaked volcano about 80 miles away.

67
00:03:48,738 --> 00:03:54,109
It came from an eruption around 7000 BC,
give or take six centuries.

68
00:03:54,176 --> 00:03:58,261
Right when the town was alive.
Someone here may well have watched it.

69
00:03:58,328 --> 00:04:03,044
But other archaeologists look at the
same picture and see a leopard skin.

70
00:04:03,111 --> 00:04:05,879
Leopards really do appear on these walls.

71
00:04:05,946 --> 00:04:08,254
So the jury is still out.

72
00:04:08,321 --> 00:04:11,336
Now the part
they don't put on the postcards.

73
00:04:11,679 --> 00:04:13,767
The floors were swept clean.

74
00:04:13,834 --> 00:04:18,670
But tests of the walls and floors
found traces of human and animal dung.

75
00:04:18,737 --> 00:04:22,492
Rubbish heaps and animal pens
sat right beside the houses.

76
00:04:22,559 --> 00:04:24,919
The soil holds parasite eggs.

77
00:04:24,986 --> 00:04:29,667
Up to a third of the early townspeople
show infections on their bones.

78
00:04:29,734 --> 00:04:32,381
Verdict: you survive your house.

79
00:04:32,448 --> 00:04:35,391
Smoky, itchy, and never alone.

80
00:04:35,546 --> 00:04:38,590
Third question. Do you get enough to eat?

81
00:04:38,723 --> 00:04:40,238
Make your guess.

82
00:04:41,353 --> 00:04:45,296
Remember the wild wheat on the mountain
at the end of our last film?

83
00:04:45,496 --> 00:04:47,834
Here its descendants are a crop.

84
00:04:47,901 --> 00:04:52,120
Einkorn and emmer wheat, with barley,
peas and lentils.

85
00:04:52,187 --> 00:04:56,183
You grind the grain on a stone,
and make bread and porridge.

86
00:04:56,250 --> 00:05:00,612
Your family keeps sheep and goats.
People still hunt wild animals,

87
00:05:00,679 --> 00:05:03,692
and the art on the walls
is full of the chase.

88
00:05:03,759 --> 00:05:07,601
Here's a surprise.
Women and men ate the same food.

89
00:05:07,668 --> 00:05:09,957
The chemistry of their bones matches.

90
00:05:10,024 --> 00:05:14,172
In many later farming villages in Europe,
some men ate better.

91
00:05:14,239 --> 00:05:17,201
But soft bread every day has a price.

92
00:05:17,268 --> 00:05:18,341
Your teeth.

93
00:05:18,408 --> 00:05:21,984
About one adult tooth in ten has a cavity.

94
00:05:22,051 --> 00:05:24,758
And the food keeps getting farther away.

95
00:05:24,825 --> 00:05:29,485
Over the centuries, fields and herds
moved farther out across the plain.

96
00:05:29,552 --> 00:05:30,983
So did the firewood.

97
00:05:31,050 --> 00:05:32,454
The leg bones show it.

98
00:05:32,521 --> 00:05:35,740
Later townspeople walked
much more than the first ones.

99
00:05:35,807 --> 00:05:37,033
Children too.

100
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:41,485
Verdict. You eat. You walk.
You lose a tooth or two.

101
00:05:41,576 --> 00:05:45,130
Fourth question.
Do you survive your neighbours?

102
00:05:45,262 --> 00:05:46,776
Make your guess.

103
00:05:47,815 --> 00:05:51,760
Scientists examined 93
skulls from the town.

104
00:05:51,827 --> 00:05:55,639
25 of them had been hit on the head,
and healed.

105
00:05:55,706 --> 00:05:57,457
More than one in four.

106
00:05:57,524 --> 00:06:02,239
Twelve had been hit more than once.
Some of them up to five times.

107
00:06:02,306 --> 00:06:05,856
More women than men.
And the women hit most often

108
00:06:05,923 --> 00:06:08,973
took the blows on the top
or the back of the head.

109
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:11,411
They weren't facing whoever hit them.

110
00:06:12,121 --> 00:06:13,681
The dents are round.

111
00:06:13,748 --> 00:06:18,646
And excavators found scores of hard
clay balls in and around the houses,

112
00:06:18,713 --> 00:06:20,178
about the right size.

113
00:06:20,245 --> 00:06:23,962
The researchers think they may
have been flung from slings.

114
00:06:24,029 --> 00:06:28,030
The wounds were most
common in the town's most crowded years.

115
00:06:28,097 --> 00:06:29,921
But here's the strange part.

116
00:06:29,988 --> 00:06:32,769
Nobody has found
evidence of a killing blow.

117
00:06:32,836 --> 00:06:34,679
People hurt each other here.

118
00:06:34,746 --> 00:06:37,748
As far as we can tell,
they didn't kill each other.

119
00:06:37,815 --> 00:06:42,118
Verdict: you survive,
maybe with a dent in your head.

120
00:06:42,275 --> 00:06:43,851
Before the last question,

121
00:06:43,918 --> 00:06:47,063
there's one thing from that list
we have to talk about.

122
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:48,778
In 1961,

123
00:06:48,845 --> 00:06:53,363
the first excavator, James Mellaart,
found this figure of a woman.

124
00:06:53,430 --> 00:06:58,088
Large,
seated, her hands resting on two big cats.

125
00:06:58,155 --> 00:07:02,225
He believed the town
worshipped a great Mother Goddess.

126
00:07:02,292 --> 00:07:06,584
For decades, groups of goddess
worshippers made pilgrimages here.

127
00:07:06,651 --> 00:07:09,155
Then Ian Hodder's team dug for a quarter

128
00:07:09,222 --> 00:07:13,299
of a century and found
about 2,000 figurines.

129
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:15,013
Most of them are animals.

130
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:17,969
Only a small share are clearly women.

131
00:07:18,036 --> 00:07:21,636
And the clearly female ones
show up late in the town's life,

132
00:07:21,703 --> 00:07:23,496
mostly in the rubbish heaps.

133
00:07:23,563 --> 00:07:24,961
In 2016,

134
00:07:25,028 --> 00:07:30,447
Hodder's team did find a beautiful
marble woman, polished and unbroken.

135
00:07:30,514 --> 00:07:34,900
He thought she showed an older
woman of standing, not a goddess.

136
00:07:34,967 --> 00:07:36,834
So were women in charge?

137
00:07:36,901 --> 00:07:41,330
Our last question holds the answer,
and it comes from DNA.

138
00:07:41,397 --> 00:07:45,056
Fifth question.
When you die, do you stay home?

139
00:07:45,181 --> 00:07:46,695
Make your guess.

140
00:07:47,725 --> 00:07:48,586
Yes.

141
00:07:48,653 --> 00:07:51,717
You're buried under the raised
platform in the main room,

142
00:07:51,784 --> 00:07:54,287
while your family
goes on living above you.

143
00:07:54,354 --> 00:07:58,162
Knees to chest,
often wrapped in a mat or a basket.

144
00:07:58,229 --> 00:08:01,233
Now remember those 131 people.

145
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:06,373
Their DNA came from 35 houses,
spread over a thousand years.

146
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:10,919
In the early town, the people under
one floor were mostly close family.

147
00:08:10,986 --> 00:08:12,925
About six pairs in ten.

148
00:08:12,992 --> 00:08:14,500
Then that changed.

149
00:08:14,567 --> 00:08:17,714
By the late town,
it was barely two in ten.

150
00:08:17,781 --> 00:08:21,574
Babies from different
families were buried in the same house.

151
00:08:21,641 --> 00:08:25,931
Yet the babies' own bones
suggest their mothers ate the same food,

152
00:08:25,998 --> 00:08:27,939
as if they shared one kitchen.

153
00:08:28,006 --> 00:08:31,872
The researchers think fostering
or adoption may explain it.

154
00:08:31,939 --> 00:08:34,235
Family was whoever you lived with.

155
00:08:34,302 --> 00:08:37,736
And when they were related,
it ran through the mothers.

156
00:08:37,803 --> 00:08:40,001
Daughters stayed in the house
they grew up in.

157
00:08:40,068 --> 00:08:42,579
Sons, most likely, moved away.

158
00:08:42,646 --> 00:08:47,968
Girls who died young were five times more
likely than boys to be buried with gifts.

159
00:08:48,035 --> 00:08:50,540
Beads, shells, pigment.

160
00:08:50,607 --> 00:08:54,835
In one house lay an old
woman with no relatives there at all.

161
00:08:54,902 --> 00:08:56,621
She got the place of honour,

162
00:08:56,688 --> 00:09:01,182
with boar tusks, beads,
and an anklet of imitation deer teeth.

163
00:09:01,249 --> 00:09:05,946
So was it ruled by women?
There's no sign anyone ruled at all.

164
00:09:06,013 --> 00:09:10,095
But for a thousand years,
this town followed the mothers' line.

165
00:09:10,162 --> 00:09:12,898
Verdict: you stay home forever.

166
00:09:12,965 --> 00:09:16,550
With the family you chose,
or the one that chose you.

167
00:09:16,715 --> 00:09:20,045
Born:
you made it, with scars on your teeth.

168
00:09:20,112 --> 00:09:22,651
Home: smoky, but home.

169
00:09:22,718 --> 00:09:25,608
Fed: yes, minus a tooth or two.

170
00:09:25,675 --> 00:09:30,695
Your neighbours: dented, alive.
Your dead: right under your bed.

171
00:09:30,762 --> 00:09:32,087
And the list on the right?

172
00:09:32,154 --> 00:09:37,585
Two crossed out, two still argued,
and one rewritten by DNA.

173
00:09:37,652 --> 00:09:41,007
How many did you guess right?
Tell us in the comments.

174
00:09:41,074 --> 00:09:43,731
So why is nobody living here now?

175
00:09:44,337 --> 00:09:48,236
Around 6200 BC, the climate turned.

176
00:09:48,303 --> 00:09:52,240
About 160 years of colder, drier weather.

177
00:09:52,307 --> 00:09:55,507
We know because the town's
cooking pots recorded it,

178
00:09:55,574 --> 00:09:57,791
in the fat soaked into the clay.

179
00:09:57,858 --> 00:10:01,207
People switched from cattle to tougher
sheep and goats.

180
00:10:01,274 --> 00:10:04,921
They cracked bones harder,
for every bit of marrow.

181
00:10:04,988 --> 00:10:09,490
Big shared homes gave way
to smaller families, each on their own.

182
00:10:09,557 --> 00:10:13,281
And slowly, house by house, people left.

183
00:10:13,919 --> 00:10:18,718
By 5950 BC, the old mound was empty.

184
00:10:18,785 --> 00:10:21,720
People had moved to a new
village across the stream.

185
00:10:21,787 --> 00:10:25,377
But the way of life lived on,
and it travelled.

186
00:10:25,444 --> 00:10:29,311
Farmers from Anatolia carried it west,
into Europe.

187
00:10:29,378 --> 00:10:31,097
If you have European roots,

188
00:10:31,164 --> 00:10:35,092
some of your ancestors very likely
came from villages like this one.

189
00:10:35,159 --> 00:10:36,843
And the digging isn't over.

190
00:10:36,910 --> 00:10:41,922
In 2025, a team announced a house
where nobody seems to have lived.

191
00:10:41,989 --> 00:10:46,373
Under its floor, twenty people,
probably brought there from elsewhere.

192
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:50,735
2,000 years after this town emptied,
far to the south-east,

193
00:10:50,802 --> 00:10:53,302
a settlement grows into something new.

194
00:10:53,369 --> 00:10:57,707
A real city, with temples,
kings, and the first writing.

195
00:10:57,774 --> 00:10:58,789
Uruk.

196
00:10:58,864 --> 00:11:01,164
That's where our journey goes next.

197
00:11:01,847 --> 00:11:04,554
But tonight,
when you walk through your front door,

198
00:11:04,621 --> 00:11:07,776
think of the first people
who lived this close together.

199
00:11:07,843 --> 00:11:10,357
They came home through the roof.

